    "body": "- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\nHi!\r\n\r\nFirst of all: I love your plugin suite. Thank you very much for your work!\r\n\r\nI would like to propose extending `mini-move` to allow shifting a single character left or right in linewise mode as a toggleable functionality. This is something you already documented as not implemented, so I know you already thought of if, but not if you decided to not support it by design. I hope not :-P\r\n\r\nI've added a few `NOTE:` comments in the code to call your attention to some points. Please let me know what I need to change to make this ready to be merged. Also, since this is a small change, should I just amend this commit? What's your history tidiness policy?\r\n\r\nThanks!",
    "closed_at": "2025-09-08T16:46:51Z",
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the PR!\r\n\r\nCan you please expand on what the expected usage here? If I understand correctly, you'd like, for example, to be able to press `2<A-l>` and it will move a single character two columns to the right, correct? If yes, then it is expected to be done with `v2<A-l><Esc>`. For a single column movement the simple `xP` is enough (which I use regularly), although it has some drawbacks regarding dot-repeat.",
