        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I ended up using this mapping in my config to make it behave the way I want:\r\n\r\nYep, that works. Here are couple of extra suggestions:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n-- Make a generator directly\r\nlocal function make_move_char(direction)\r\n  return function()\r\n    -- Not really necessary, but using `vim.api` functions is usually\r\n    -- preferred over `vim.fn`\r\n    vim.api.nvim_feedkeys('v', 'nx', true)\r\n    MiniMove.move_selection(direction)\r\n    vim.api.nvim_feedkeys('v', 'nx', true)\r\n  end\r\nend\r\n\r\n-- Drop the whole `{ noremap = true }` because its behavior is default for\r\n-- `vim.keymap.set()`. And prefer to use `{ remap = true }` for inverse case\r\n-- (make mappings which treats keys as if they are mapped).\r\nvim.keymap.set('n', '<A-h>', make_move_char('left'))\r\nvim.keymap.set('n', '<A-l>', make_move_char('right'))\r\n```\r\n\r\nAlso, if this type of movement is all users want, then it can (mostly; there are literal edge cases) be implemented without 'mini.move':\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nvim.keymap.set('n', '<M-l>', '<Cmd>exe \"normal! x\".v:count1.\"lP\"<CR>')\r\nvim.keymap.set('n', '<M-h>', '<Cmd>exe \"normal! x\".v:count1.\"hP\"<CR>')\r\n```",
            "created_at": "2025-09-09T08:27:58Z",
