        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> You said that _using `vim.api` functions is usually preferred over `vim.fn`_. Is the reasoning for this documented anywhere?\r\n\r\nNot to that extent, sorry. The best one is `:h lua-guide-api` which this:\r\n\r\n> Through this, any possible interaction can be done through Lua without writing a complete new API from scratch. For this reason, functions are usually not duplicated between layers unless there is a significant benefit in functionality or performance (e.g., you can map Lua functions directly through |nvim_create_autocmd()| but not through |:autocmd|). In case there are multiple ways of achieving the same thing, this guide will only cover what is most convenient to use from Lua.\r\n\r\nBut generally the idea is that:\r\n- `vim.fn` has to go through extra layer of Lua->Vimscript-Lua conversion, which is usually best avoided if not needed. See also `:h lua-vimscript`.\r\n    If I am not mistaken (which I might here), this for example results into the limitation that some `vim.fn` functions can't be called under certain conditions (like inside `vim.uv` callbacks).\r\n- The `vim.api` are specifically built by Neovim for Neovim, so usually is better.\r\n\r\nAlso, some functionality is only available through Vimscript functions (`vim.fn`) without (yet) a `vim.api` alternative (like working with folds, window state, virtual columns, etc.). So in this case `vim.fn` are necessary.\r\n\r\n**But** this is a very, *very* loose advice. In not performance critical areas use whatever feels better. I often use `vim.fn.line('.')` to get cursor line instead of `vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0)[1]`, for example.",
